#: src/features/Blog/blogData.tsx:61
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Naively, I assumed the book would give me tools - techniques for decoding "
"people, keys to reading intentions, perhaps even a method for understanding "
"the stranger in my own home. But the book is really anything but.\n"
"\n"
"It argues that our desire to understand others is so strong that we "
"convince ourselves we already do. We take fragments - tone of voice, "
"posture, reputation, context - and from them construct entire "
"personalities. When our guesses happen to be correct, we treat that as "
"proof of our skill. When they fail, we call it an exception.\n"
"\n"
"And so our confidence grows.\n"
"\n"
"But who is a “stranger”? Not just the person in the elevator or the cashier "
"at the store. A stranger can be a colleague you’ve worked with for years. A "
"friend who has shared secrets. Even someone who has quietly entered your "
"everyday life and begun to shape it.\n"
"\n"
"We learn a few details. We hear a few confessions. We observe a few "
"patterns. Then we exhale, believing we have understood. What we rarely "
"notice is how many blanks we have filled in ourselves - motives, "
"intentions, inner states.\n"
"\n"
"We can mistake coherence for truth.\n"
"\n"
"The book contains unsettling examples. One of them centers on a respected "
"figure in the financial world - a man with status, reputation, elite "
"connections, institutional credibility. For decades, investors trusted him. "
"Major players invested. Returns appeared stable. Everything about him "
"signaled reliability.\n"
"\n"
"Behind that facade stood one of the largest financial frauds in history: "
"the scheme of Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"It seems that his operation lasted so long because people felt safe. "
"Reputation became a substitute for verification. Social proof handled "
"skepticism. When millions trust someone, doubt begins to feel almost "
"irrational - even embarrassing.\n"
"\n"
"It becomes easier to assume that someone else has already asked the hard "
"questions."
msgstr ""
"Naively, I assumed the book would give me tools - techniques for decoding "
"people, keys to reading intentions, perhaps even a method for understanding "
"the stranger in my own home. But the book is really anything but.\n"
"\n"
"Naively, I assumed that the book would provide me with tools - techniques "
"for reading people, keys to understanding intentions, perhaps even a method "
"to understand the stranger in my own home. But the book is really anything "
"like that.\n"
"\n"
"It argues that our desire to understand others is so strong that we "
"convince ourselves we already do. We take fragments - tone of voice, "
"posture, reputation, context - and from them construct entire "
"personalities. When our guesses happen to be correct, we treat that as "
"proof of our skill. When they fail, we call it an exception.\n"
"\n"
"Twierdzi, że nasza chęć zrozumienia innych jest tak silna, że przekonujemy "
"samych siebie, że już to potrafimy. Bierzemy fragmenty - ton głosu, "
"postawę, reputację, kontekst - i na ich podstawie konstruujemy całe "
"osobowości. Kiedy nasze przypuszczenia okazują się trafne, traktujemy to "
"jako dowód naszej umiejętności. Kiedy zawodzą, nazywamy je wyjątkiem.\n"
"\n"
"And so our confidence grows.\n"
"\n"
"I tak nasza pewność siebie rośnie.\n"
"\n"
"But who is a “stranger”? Not just the person in the elevator or the cashier "
"at the store. A stranger can be a colleague you’ve worked with for years. A "
"friend who has shared secrets. Even someone who has quietly entered your "
"everyday life and begun to shape it.\n"
"\n"
"Ale kim jest \"obcy\"? Nie tylko osoba w windzie czy kasjer w sklepie. "
"Obcym może być kolega, z którym pracujesz od lat. Przyjaciel, z którym "
"dzieliłeś się sekretami. Nawet ktoś, kto cicho wkroczył do twojego "
"codziennego życia i zaczął je kształtować.\n"
"\n"
"We learn a few details. We hear a few confessions. We observe a few "
"patterns. Then we exhale, believing we have understood. What we rarely "
"notice is how many blanks we have filled in ourselves - motives, "
"intentions, inner states.\n"
"\n"
"Poznajemy kilka szczegółów. Słyszymy kilka wyznań. Obserwujemy kilka "
"wzorców. Następnie wydychamy, wierząc, że zrozumieliśmy. Rzadko zauważamy, "
"ile puste miejsca sami wypełniliśmy - motywami, intencjami, stanami "
"wewnętrznymi.\n"
"\n"
"We can mistake coherence for truth.\n"
"\n"
"Możemy pomylić spójność z prawdą.\n"
"\n"
"The book contains unsettling examples. One of them centers on a respected "
"figure in the financial world - a man with status, reputation, elite "
"connections, institutional credibility. For decades, investors trusted him. "
"Major players invested. Returns appeared stable. Everything about him "
"signaled reliability.\n"
"\n"
"Książka zawiera niepokojące przykłady. Jeden z nich koncentruje się na "
"szanowanej postaci w świecie finansów - mężczyźnie z uznaniem, reputacją, "
"elitarnymi kontaktami, instytucjonalną wiarygodnością. Przez "
"dziesięciolecia inwestorzy mu ufali. Inwestowali w niego główni gracze. "
"Wyniki wydawały się stabilne. Wszystko w nim sygnalizowało niezawodność.\n"
"\n"
"Behind that facade stood one of the largest financial frauds in history: "
"the scheme of Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"Za tą fasadą kryła się jedna z największych oszustw finansowych w historii: "
"plan Berniego Madoffa.\n"
"\n"
"It seems that his operation lasted so long because people felt safe. "
"Reputation became a substitute for verification. Social proof handled "
"skepticism. When millions trust someone, doubt begins to feel almost "
"irrational - even embarrassing.\n"
"\n"
"Wygląda na to, że jego operacja trwała tak długo, ponieważ ludzie czuli się "
"bezpieczni. Reputacja stała się substytutem weryfikacji. Społeczny dowód "
"radził sobie ze sceptycyzmem. Kiedy miliony ufają komuś, wątpliwość zaczyna "
"wydawać się niemal irracjonalna - wręcz żenująca.\n"
"\n"
"It becomes easier to assume that someone else has already asked the hard "
"questions.\n"
"\n"
"Łatwiej jest założyć, że ktoś inny już zadał trudne pytania."

